      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "The film sparked a wave of visually-driven environmental documentaries that eschew traditional narration. <a href=\"/movie/54527\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"54527\">Into Eternity</a> (2010) uses haunting imagery of nuclear waste facilities to contemplate humanity's legacy. <a href=\"/movie/127962\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"127962\">Leviathan</a> (2012) straps cameras to fishing boats and workers to create an immersive portrait of industrial fishing. These films trust in cinema's visual power to convey complex ideas about humanity's relationship with nature. SUBHEAD: The Art of Industrial Photography"
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Burtynsky's large-format photographs of quarries, mines, and factories influenced a generation of photographers and filmmakers. River and Tides (2001) similarly follows artist Andy Goldsworthy, while <a href=\"/movie/265297\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"265297\">The Salt of the Earth</a> (2014) profiles photographer Sebastião Salgado's documentation of human impact on landscapes. These films explore how still images can capture the massive scale of environmental change."
      }
